WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.922 --> 00:00:06.880
GABRIELA VANAKEN: My name
is Gabriella VanAken,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.750
and I'm a senior biochemistry
major here at Hillsdale.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.900
I'm honored to introduce our
speaker for today, Dr. Arthur

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.400
Herman.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.940
Dr. Herman is a senior fellow
at the Hudson Institute

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.910
and director of its
Quantum Alliance Initiative

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:25.180
where he works on quantum
computing and related quantum

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.490
information technology.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.880
He received his PhD in
history from the John Hopkins

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.090
University.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.580
Dr. Herman has been a
professor of history

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.420
at Georgetown University,
Catholic University,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:44.340
George Mason University, and
the University of the South.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.780
He writes regularly for
Commentary, Foreign Policy,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.090
The American Interest,
Mosaic, Nikkei Asian Review,

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.370
and The Wall Street Journal.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.210
He is the author
of several books,

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.420
including Gandhi and Churchill--

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.720
The Epic Rivalry That Destroyed
An Empire and Forged Our

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:09.140
Age, which was a finalist for
the Pulitzer Prize in 2009,

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.850
1917, Lenin, Wilson, and
the Birth of the New World

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.880
Disorder, and
Douglas MacArthur--

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.440
American Warrior.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.768
Please join me in welcoming
Dr. Arthur Herman.

00:01:21.768 --> 00:01:26.214
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:33.315 --> 00:01:35.690
ARTHUR HERMAN: Well, thank
you very much for a very sweet

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.610
introduction.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.520
And hi, how are you?

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.712
It's been a great
session, don't you think?

00:01:44.712 --> 00:01:45.920
The lectures have been great.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.600
The lectures have
been wonderful,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.520
the food and the drink has been
excellent, and it has been,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:00.860
I think, a really wonderful tour
of America's leading military

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.780
heroes.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.860
And I know that we're--
all of us-- very,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:09.259
very grateful that Alan Guelzo
was able to finish his speech

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:10.573
from last night.

00:02:10.573 --> 00:02:15.780
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.270
You gave us a bit of a scare
there, my friend, last night,

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.830
but it's really great to
have Alan back with us and up

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.900
and operating.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.250
What I'm going to be doing--

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.770
and what's going to
happen today and tonight--

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:32.940
is we are now going
to nudge you none

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.240
too gently into
the 20th century,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.260
into the violent century,
the most violent century

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.870
probably in human history.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:46.530
And we'll be talking
about two figures.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:50.160
I will be talking about one,
and then Victor Davis Hanson

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.810
we'll be talking about another
who spring out of that century

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.437
and out of the conflicts
that are involved.

00:02:57.437 --> 00:02:59.520
But I think what you'll
see-- and I will certainly

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.440
say this for the person
I'll be talking about,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.660
Douglas MacArthur--
is you will definitely

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.560
see traces of that
earlier century,

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.000
of a more courteous age, of
an age which place values

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.540
on things such as honor,
courage, dedication

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.310
to duty, and dedication to
country and God that reflect

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.340
those earlier
traditions in ways that

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.465
are really quite striking and
quite amazing to think about.

00:03:27.465 --> 00:03:29.340
So I'm going to talk
about Douglas MacArthur,

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.390
the subject of my 2016 book.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:37.920
And what I want to
do is to start out

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.380
with understanding
just, shall we say,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.070
a quick overview
of his biography

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:50.040
and of the remarkable
man that he was.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.750
But, as always,
when we're talking

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.550
about the character of
any person, of any man,

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.280
the place to start
is with the father.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.520
And you're not going to
understand Douglas MacArthur,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.350
the man, unless you understand
Arthur MacArthur, the father.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.050
Here he is.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:14.370
Every time I see this picture
of him in his tennis uniform,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:19.500
first, you think like it's a
kid dressed up for a military.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.170
What happened there?

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:24.030
[INAUDIBLE]

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.060
You think it's a kid dressed
up for a Halloween costume

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.150
or for a costume ball.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.730
And that is until you look
into the eyes and the mouth

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.380
of the man that's
standing there,

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:38.720
this 16-year-old here who has
enlisted in the 24th Wisconsin

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.700
regiment and who quickly rises
from basically an adjutant role

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:48.800
to, finally, by the
end of his service

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.800
during the Civil
War to full Colonel.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.020
It's a service which earns
him two serious wounds,

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:03.320
earns him the Medal of Honor
at the siege of Chattanooga

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.230
with the taking of Lookout
Mountain in which he leads

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:10.910
his regiment, the 24th
Wisconsin, up the mountain,

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.030
carrying his regimental flag.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:18.950
And it's said that what he cried
out is leading his troops up--

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:22.283
on Wisconsin became
the state motto.

00:05:22.283 --> 00:05:24.450
And I know there is some
of you here from Wisconsin.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.600
So that's definitely
information that you're

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.650
going to want to write
down and take note of.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:35.790
But a man whose bravery under
fire, whose skill and command,

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.420
and whose leadership
in battle was such

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:42.740
that by the end of the war,
he had risen to full Colonel,

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:45.110
even though he was still
not old enough to vote.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:53.270
Against all family advice, he
decided to remain in the Army

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:56.930
at the war's end, even
as the nation demobilized

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.750
and the number of position
and posts in which

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:04.500
to serve in the US Army
drastically diminished.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:09.740
So he ended up taking
himself and his family--

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:15.620
his wife, who was
actually a southern lady,

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:19.130
a kind of real-life
version of Scarlett O'Hara,

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:23.960
in many ways, who became
known by her family nickname

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.160
of Pinky.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.020
It will be very important
to the formation.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.490
Why does this keep
bumping up one slide more?

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:34.380
That's not what I want.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.270
Oh, there we go.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:40.100
Where was I?

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.770
Oh, yeah, talking
about the family--

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.950
to a series of
remote Army outposts.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:51.430
In fact, the life that you see
of your average US Army cavalry

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:54.520
or infantry captain on the
frontier in the John Ford

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:58.120
movies, that was Arthur
MacArthur's life.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:01.480
That was the life he
led in 1870s and 1880s.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:05.800
It's the life into which Douglas
MacArthur was born in an Army

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.130
barracks in Arkansas
and the life

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:13.030
that he would dedicate himself
to for the rest of his--

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.280
until the very end
of his life here

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.400
because he very quickly followed
in his father's footsteps,

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.330
his revered father's
footsteps, into the Army.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:27.510
He would go to West Point,
where he was heavily

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:31.840
involved in both
athletics and academics.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:37.410
We see him here as manager
of the Harvard football team.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.000
That's him.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.790
Why does it do that, guys?

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:41.922
I don't want to move--

00:07:41.922 --> 00:07:44.130
I want to stay at the one
slide till I'm ready to go.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:47.434
If there's a way to fix
that, that'd be great.

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:53.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:53.830
Even better.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.640
Do we lose it altogether,
or are we coming back?

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:09.850
Anyway, his time at
West Point with such

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:16.780
that he was, without doubt,
the most accomplished graduate

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.570
of West Point since
Robert E. Lee.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.030
The academic records
that he scored

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:26.500
would stand as records
for years and decades

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.530
to come in the course of that.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:30.850
Then when we get our
pictures back, what we'll do

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:35.559
is we'll see him as he makes
his move into active service

00:08:35.559 --> 00:08:37.510
during the First World War.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:39.100
And it's during
that active service

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:45.220
that he will be awarded no
less than seven silver stars

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.580
in action in combat
there and will

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.090
rise to the rank of Brigadier
General, the youngest

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.910
Brigadier General
in the US Army,

00:08:54.910 --> 00:09:00.880
and eventually even to
Major General in spite

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.230
of a long-standing
record of insubordination

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:09.130
and disobedience to orders to
superiors, which he establishes

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.230
first during the First
World War and then

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:14.090
goes on to become part of his
pattern of life going out.

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:17.350
We can talk more about
that a little bit.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:24.070
Then after World War I, he's
asked to take over his alma

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.400
mater, West Point, where
he becomes the youngest

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:31.930
superintendent of West Point
in its history and carries out

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:36.370
a series of reforms of
the curriculum to update

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:39.550
the institution from its
19th-century roots and from

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:42.580
a curriculum that Robert
E. Lee or Ulysses S.

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:45.400
Grant would have recognized
to one that it's now going

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:47.580
to include-- there we are--

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.680
there he is getting his
Distinguished Service Cross

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.680
from General Pershing.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.870
They didn't get along.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.750
There were constant
conflicts between them.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.670
Pershing was also competing
for the affections of the woman

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:11.180
that MacArthur would
eventually marry.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:15.680
That added an extra shall we say
juicy dimension to the rivalry

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.580
between the two men.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.630
But even Pershing, for all
of his dislike for what

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:26.570
he saw as MacArthur's arrogance,
for his affected airs,

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:30.560
for his inability it seems to
straightforwardly follow orders

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.330
that he were given
had to admit he

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.580
was a first-rate officer
and a first-rate commander

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:38.930
and one of the bravest
men many ever met.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:42.500
A testimony, by the way, which
he shared with General George

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:46.010
Patton when Patton got to know
him in the First World War

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:49.377
and saw him under action, wrote
back to his family and said,

00:10:49.377 --> 00:10:50.960
this is the bravest
man I've ever seen

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.287
in my life, Douglas MacArthur.

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:59.780
So there he is as
superintendent at West Point,

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.980
then after that goes on to a
series of other military posts,

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:08.000
and takes off time in 1928 to
become president of the Olympic

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.150
Committee and takes the
American Olympic team

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:15.980
to the Olympic games
in the Netherlands.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:19.790
This was a landmark event in
the history of the Olympics,

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:23.780
by the way, because it really
was Douglas MacArthur who

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:27.590
created the modern
American Olympic Committee

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.240
and effort in it.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:32.548
He was determined that
in the course of what

00:11:32.548 --> 00:11:35.090
happened in those games that
the United States would come out

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:37.530
ahead and take the lead in it.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:39.920
And so he took a personal
interest in everything

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:43.790
that happened and unfolded
in the course of the games.

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:48.440
His motto that he gave to his
coaches and to the participants

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:51.230
all the way through is,
Americans never quit.

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:53.930
This is a show of
who we are, what

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:56.600
happens in the Olympic
games, and how many medals we

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:57.170
carried away.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.180
And, in fact, they carried
away a record number

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:02.030
of medals, 24 of
them, more, in fact,

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.870
gold medals, more gold medals
than the next two countries

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.420
combined, Germany and Finland.

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:12.980
They set 17 Olympic
records at the 1928 games,

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.760
American athletes did,
and seven world records.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:20.090
And American Olympic effort
would never be the same again.

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:24.350
He set a standard for what
American Olympic performance

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:27.470
and American sports, in
general, would have to achieve.

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:30.540
There was embodied
in American sports

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.930
an aspect of American
character that

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.840
was expressed through
winning and through competing

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:38.070
at the highest possible level.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.660
It's one of the aspects
of Douglas MacArthur

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:45.050
that he brings to the cultural
table of the United States,

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:46.760
as well as to his
military career.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.560
Then comes an outbreak
in World War II, when

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.590
he is in the Philippines,
commanding US forces

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:59.010
there after being
bottlenecked there

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:02.670
by the surprise Japanese
attack and making

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:05.070
a dramatic escape
from Corregidor

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:10.770
to Australia to regroup and to
rearm the United States to take

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.810
back the Philippines
and the other parts

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:16.350
of the Southwest Pacific
theater which he's engaged in.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:20.580
He manages in 1944 to
fulfill his promise

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:23.850
that he made to the Filipinos
before he left Corregidor.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:24.840
I shall return.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:28.140
And he does return
in October 1944

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:33.590
with a dramatic landing
at late day near Tacloban

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:34.650
on the eastern coast.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:38.130
And he makes this dramatic
radio announcement

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.990
to the citizens
of the Philippines

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:43.110
that he has returned and that a
time has come for them to rally

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:47.490
against their Japanese occupiers
and that freedom has finally

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:48.090
come.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:53.880
The 4,000-mile trek of return
back to the Philippines

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:58.600
and then to push on
to Japan is complete.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:03.650
Then after Japan's surrender,
he will again participate

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.200
in one of the major events--

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:07.880
starting points of
the postwar world

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:10.760
when he arrives
at Atzugi Airport

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:14.240
in order to receive
the Japanese surrender.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:18.290
And this moment became one
of the most important really

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:21.620
for the development of the
American occupation in Japan

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:24.770
in the entire post-war period
because, as you can see

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:29.360
as MacArthur descends down the
staircase here from the plane--

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.070
from his private
plane, which the baton

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:36.630
which he had arrived
from the Philippines.

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:38.930
He arrived unarmed
carrying no sidearms

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.960
and insisted that everyone in
his party carry no sidearms.

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:43.730
This was at a time
when there was still

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:47.000
enormous fear about
Japanese snipers at large

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.000
in the country.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.630
He was even warned not
to land at Atzugi at all

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.950
without a massive armed escort.

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:54.440
They said, do you
realize there is

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:58.810
a training camp for banzai
pilots here at Atzugi?

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:01.340
We've got 8,000
banzai pilots here

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:03.200
who are just
waiting for a chance

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.720
to get their hands on
you if they possibly can.

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:06.925
You're crazy to come here.

00:15:06.925 --> 00:15:08.300
You're crazy at
least not to come

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:12.050
without a massive military
escort in the process.

00:15:12.050 --> 00:15:13.950
He said, no, we're
going to go do it.

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:16.820
We're going to go
unarmed in the process.

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:22.340
It was part of the step, part
of the psychological operation

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:27.950
that MacArthur was
engaged in post-war Japan

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:31.600
to make the Japanese realize
that the United States was

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:35.150
there not as occupiers,
but as partners

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:38.630
in building a new Japan
and a new partnership

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:41.300
in Asia, which was,
of course, very much

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:45.950
reflected also in the major
ceremony, which took place

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:49.320
at the signing of the
surrender terms for Japan

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:51.500
and for the Allied
nations, which

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:58.070
MacArthur presided over and
announced over worldwide hookup

00:15:58.070 --> 00:15:59.510
on radio.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:04.190
And when the signing of
the treaty was completed,

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:09.560
MacArthur was able to announce
that the hostilities have

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:12.600
ceased, not that the
Japanese have surrendered.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:14.780
But hostilities have ceased.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:20.780
All of the Japanese delegates at
the signing recognized at once.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.750
It was translated to what he
was saying what it really meant.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:26.360
And that was again that
this was a celebration

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:30.830
not of Japan's surrender and
humiliation, but of a new era--

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:33.830
new era for Asia,
a new era for Japan

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:37.400
and a new set of
relationships for US and Japan

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:39.650
partnership in the
future, which is really

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:43.340
what MacArthur's role
as then overseeing

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:45.560
the occupation of
Japan really involved.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:53.380
Well, the story doesn't end
there because five years--

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:54.940
less than five
years-- after the end

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.690
of the war, North Korean
forces invade South

00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:02.190
Korea on the 25th of June 1950.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.050
And MacArthur is put
in the role of trying

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:07.690
to reverse this
desperate situation

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:09.609
and to prevent
catastrophe and the loss

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:14.170
of the entire Korean
peninsula to the communists.

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:16.510
And so he conceives this plan--

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:19.390
this brilliant
plan-- of a surprise

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:23.270
amphibious landing at Inchon,
which all of the experts

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:24.912
says it will not work.

00:17:24.912 --> 00:17:26.079
This is not going to happen.

00:17:26.079 --> 00:17:28.240
You understand
everything is against you

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:30.880
in carrying this kind of
thing out-- the meteorological

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:34.390
conditions, the tides.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:37.010
It simply is not
going to succeed.

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:39.490
And he goes ahead and
goes ahead and does it,

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:42.520
despite even objections from
the Joint Chiefs of Staff.

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:44.440
He persuades them
that he knows what

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.660
he's doing, that
destiny will not

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:49.760
let him fail at this endeavor.

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:55.480
And so in September of 1950,
he leads the amphibious landing

00:17:55.480 --> 00:18:01.210
at Inchon, and it completely
reverses the course of the war

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:04.870
and enables the
Allied forces, the UN

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:08.140
forces not only to recapture
Seoul, the capital of South

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:11.710
Korea, but also to capture
Pyongyang, the capital of North

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:15.220
Korea, and to bring
the possibility

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:20.200
of the unification of
Korea into as close

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:23.840
to reality as it has ever been.

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:25.300
So that's the man.

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:26.440
That's a hell of a record.

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:29.770
That's a hell of a resume
when you think about it--

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:33.670
a leader, a war commander, a
general in three world wars--

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:38.110
World War I and Korea
and the Cold War--

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:44.830
a man whose military record
is one that involves not only

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:48.880
enormous bravery and
courage under fire,

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:50.980
but also includes
one of the most

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:54.370
important strategic
operations ever

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:56.770
conducted by an American
military commander,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.630
the landing at Inchon.

00:18:58.630 --> 00:19:01.120
And you can include other
of his decisions made

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:05.560
during the war in
the Pacific as also

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:09.820
fitting into the operations
of a true strategic genius,

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:14.560
I think, in the carrying
out of his war record

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.650
and of his war accomplishments.

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:18.700
So I should be done.

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.840
I mean, that takes
care of the whole case.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:22.430
But there is a problem here.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:25.930
And the problem is that when
we look at Douglas MacArthur's

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:30.010
record in that way and we lay
it out decade by decade war

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:32.460
by war, it's hugely impressive.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:34.690
It's stellar.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:40.720
So why is Douglas MacArthur's
reputation so lousy?

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:45.650
Why is it that for decades,
he's been looked down upon?

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:49.490
He's been derided,
been traduced,

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:53.680
been subjected to any
number of types of attacks

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:59.440
and of slurs on
his character, even

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:04.720
on his own physical bravery.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:07.540
Many stories, for
example, rumors

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:09.940
that go around about his
escape from Corregidor

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:15.940
was a means by which he was able
to avoid the possibility of--

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:21.430
abandon his troops and fled
out of fear of physical danger,

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:25.120
the idea promoted by a
contemporary historian,

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:30.370
by the way, that,
in fact, that he

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:34.060
bribed the president
of the Philippines

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:36.940
in order to made them pay
a bribe in order to escape

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.600
from Corregidor himself.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:40.900
I've heard one story--

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:43.300
I was told this when I was
working in the MacArthur

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:45.640
archives in Norfolk, Virginia--

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:49.930
that he chose the place
for the landing at Leyte

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:51.580
in the liberation
of the Philippines

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:56.060
at Tacloban because his
mistress had a house there.

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:59.050
And that this was, in fact,
why he chose the landing.

00:20:59.050 --> 00:21:01.480
Douglas MacArthur
didn't have a mistress.

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:02.890
There never was a mistress.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.290
It's a completely made-up
story, and yet people

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:07.700
make these kinds of
stories up all the time.

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:09.430
And even that photograph
that I started

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:13.960
with of his landing at Leyte
there, even the stories

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:16.930
that, in fact, that
was all staged,

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:23.440
that was rehearsed, or even done
on and filmed on a safe beach

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.960
somewhere, even before he
arrived in the Philippines,

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:26.560
and so on.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:27.850
It's not true.

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:28.980
It's not true.

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:31.630
What the film showed and
what the photographs show

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:33.390
is what really happened.

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:37.240
The damn amphibious vehicle
got stuck on a sandbar,

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:41.230
and they had to get off
and water up to their hips

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:42.220
and jump out.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:44.980
MacArthur had done it
before in other landings

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:46.285
in the southwest Pacific too.

00:21:46.285 --> 00:21:47.410
It was nothing new for him.

00:21:51.055 --> 00:21:52.930
But given the fact that
this was taking place

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:55.900
while the beach was
still under fire

00:21:55.900 --> 00:22:00.220
and where snipers were still
100 yards away, taking potshots

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:02.260
at anything that
moved on the beach,

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:05.780
it was, again, a story
almost too good to be true.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:08.610
And I think that's true of
a lot of MacArthur's career.

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:11.030
It's almost too good to be true.

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:13.750
And I think that's one
reason why, in many ways,

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:16.900
the efforts to
denigrate his career

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:22.060
and to smear his
character tend to stick

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:23.680
because he does seem
to be almost too

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:26.470
good to be true as a figure.

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:30.350
So what I want to do is I want
to spend some time talking

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:35.620
a little bit about why I think
Douglas MacArthur has been

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:38.890
treated as
discourtesly as he has

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:43.180
and why that stands out, in many
ways, as a part of his career,

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:46.300
compared to his other
generals in World War II.

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:47.710
I mean, look at Patton--

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:52.390
Patton gets a great movie with
George C. Scott and a fantastic

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:55.960
soundtrack with it too
and George Marshall,

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:01.570
who's also revered as a sort
of demigod, in many ways,

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:04.930
as a general, and
Eisenhower who,

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:09.040
again, treated with reverence
and with enormous respect,

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:10.150
even by Hollywood.

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:14.200
I mean, if anybody had seen the
Tom Selleck portrayal of Dwight

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:17.920
Eisenhower in the days
leading up to D-day--

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:20.030
it's a wonderful
movie, by the way--

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:23.500
but it's a great
portrayal of Eisenhower

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:26.470
as being a man to be
admired, a man dealing

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:28.073
with difficult situations.

00:23:28.073 --> 00:23:29.240
And what does MacArthur get?

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:30.500
He gets a movie.

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:32.920
He's portrayed by Gregory Peck.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.440
It's a terrible movie
if you've ever seen it.

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:37.840
It's based on the William
Manchester book, which

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:40.810
has enormous biography,
which has enormous flaws

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:41.690
and many problems.

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:43.148
In fact, one of
the reasons I wrote

00:23:43.148 --> 00:23:45.820
the book was because I got
impatient with the Manchester

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:47.830
book and its treatment
of MacArthur,

00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:49.780
especially in the
later years here.

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:54.970
And the soundtrack
is hideous as well--

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:57.130
just not up to standards.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:23:57.708
So why?

00:23:57.708 --> 00:23:58.750
Why does all this happen?

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:00.167
Well, let me give
you what I think

00:24:00.167 --> 00:24:02.300
are some answers for this.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:05.620
The first is that
he is a man who

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:09.280
lived on the edge of the
unconventional in everything

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:09.940
that he did.

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:14.410
Everything he did was a slap
in the face to the commonplace

00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:16.190
and to the standard
way of operating,

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:19.203
including in his own
military service.

00:24:19.203 --> 00:24:20.620
And when we think
of him, there he

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:24.190
is with his trademark
pipe and hat

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:28.120
with a gold braid, which
he had designed himself

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:35.260
while he was field marshal of
the Philippine land forces.

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:39.850
If we actually turn the clock
back about 30 years to 1914,

00:24:39.850 --> 00:24:43.120
when he was a part of the
Army landings at Vera Cruz,

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:44.810
he's already there.

00:24:44.810 --> 00:24:45.620
That's him.

00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:46.250
That's him.

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:49.390
He's got the even got the
corncob pipe at this point

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:50.950
as a young lieutenant.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:54.760
And there he is with
his letter sweater

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:58.300
from West Point
and his suspenders

00:24:58.300 --> 00:25:01.310
and the tie around
his neck and so on.

00:25:01.310 --> 00:25:03.640
That is not standard
US Army uniform-- just

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:06.766
want to let you know.

00:25:06.766 --> 00:25:09.820
And it's at the same
time, it's by the way,

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:12.490
at that same expedition
that he's there

00:25:12.490 --> 00:25:16.040
that he performs one of
his most amazing feats

00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:19.930
and exploits of bravery,
venturing into in enemy

00:25:19.930 --> 00:25:24.580
territory in order to
make a reconnaissance

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:29.590
about the railroad stock that
the Mexican Army had on hand

00:25:29.590 --> 00:25:34.210
that had to be blown up
that was clearly recommended

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:35.200
for a medal of honor.

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:36.830
There was no doubt about it.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:40.960
But there's also no doubt
that this guy, this kid

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:43.720
is someone who is not going
to get a medal of honor,

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:46.690
not when you act like this,
not when you behave like that.

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:49.060
Even if you're Arthur
MacArthur's son,

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:53.140
still, there are limits to what
we're going to put up with.

00:25:53.140 --> 00:25:55.210
And it was just something
about Douglas MacArthur

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:57.790
that grated on
conventional minds

00:25:57.790 --> 00:26:04.610
and the standard-issue military
might, and that never changed.

00:26:04.610 --> 00:26:08.440
That was going to be true in
World War I, for example too.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:15.700
There he is, by the way, second
figure on the left in combat

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:18.050
and going over the
top in trench warfare.

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:19.837
He never wore a helmet.

00:26:19.837 --> 00:26:21.420
No one could get him
to wear a helmet.

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:23.550
He was always wearing
that strange forage

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:26.640
cap of his, which was
actually Army regulation

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:31.740
cap from which he had pulled
out the iron brace from inside--

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:33.150
the one that keeps it erect--

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:36.190
so it had that sort of
crushed, flattened look

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:37.560
that went with it.

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:40.680
Probably picked that up from
some of the Royal Flying corps

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:45.870
officers who had a sort
of a casual dashing look

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:49.330
to it, at the same time.

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:54.270
The young American aviator
by the name of Billy Mitchell

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:56.590
came by to see him and
saw the cap and said,

00:26:56.590 --> 00:26:58.500
I want one of those too.

00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:03.850
So he pulled out the iron
brace from his cap as well.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:07.500
And it became, again, sort of
the standard look for our Army

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.200
aviators right through
to World War II.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:13.200
If you look at that
hat and associate it

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:16.830
with the cockpit on
a B-17, for example,

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:20.130
you've got yourself a
scene from 12 o'clock high.

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:22.200
That's where that
hat and that's where

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:25.130
that costume came through--

00:27:25.130 --> 00:27:29.297
from Douglas MacArthur
via Billy Mitchell.

00:27:29.297 --> 00:27:31.630
But everything about him in
that-- he's a staff officer,

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:33.240
but he goes over the top--

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.220
goes over the top with
the regular troops

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:37.560
because he says he
needs to see what's

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:39.000
really happening on the ground.

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.520
He can't learn what's
really taking place

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:43.800
in the fighting and the terrain.

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:45.840
If he's going to stay
back at headquarters,

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:49.410
he's got to be in the front
but doesn't wear a helmet,

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:52.688
doesn't carry a weapon, no
sidearm, couldn't be bothered.

00:27:52.688 --> 00:27:54.480
He says, why do I want
something like that?

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:56.040
Just get in the way.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:59.580
He doesn't carry a
gas mask bag either.

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:01.620
And, in fact, he's
going to be very badly

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:05.970
gassed during the fighting
in the Argonne forest

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:10.680
as a result of that-- pays
for that little quirk of his.

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.330
But this is the Douglas
MacArthur who evolves.

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:18.630
This is one who is constantly
defying the standard-issue view

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:20.340
of how he should act
and how he should

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:22.770
behave under all circumstances.

00:28:22.770 --> 00:28:26.480
And the iconic Douglas MacArthur
that we know from World War II

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:33.370
and from Korea is
really a man who

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:37.700
is the product of that
unconventionality.

00:28:37.700 --> 00:28:41.680
The second reason I think why
MacArthur's reputation is not

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:43.510
perhaps as high as it is--

00:28:43.510 --> 00:28:45.258
and it's not unexpected,
given the fact

00:28:45.258 --> 00:28:47.800
that he is someone who defies
the conventional and everything

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:48.820
else--

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:51.890
is the fact that he never
hesitates to tell superiors

00:28:51.890 --> 00:28:53.140
what he really thinks of them.

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:59.670
And this is going to get
you in trouble, by the way,

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:03.560
including in institutions
like the United States Army

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:04.250
if you do that.

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:06.080
But, again, it's an
irrepressible part

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:11.060
of MacArthur's ways of doing
things, starting with General

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:14.090
Pershing during World
War I and then continuing

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:17.300
on when he becomes chief
of staff for the US Army

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:21.530
under Franklin Roosevelt. And
you have this picture of them

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:24.890
here, and they're sort
of looking very chummy

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:26.690
and sharing a jovial moment.

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:29.840
There weren't a lot of
those between Roosevelt

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:31.190
and MacArthur.

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:34.910
MacArthur all through
the 1920s and 1930s

00:29:34.910 --> 00:29:37.550
was absolutely convinced
that the United States

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:41.600
military budget had been cut too
much, that it had been sliced

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:45.620
to the bone and then some,
and he has constant battles,

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:48.560
both with Congress and with
the White House about ways

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:58.040
in which to raise the Army
budgets and authorizations

00:29:58.040 --> 00:30:00.020
for funds, after
one particularly

00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:04.550
bruising confrontation with
Roosevelt in the White House.

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:08.930
In fact, MacArthur, when he
stepped out onto the White

00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:11.720
House steps, threw up.

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:15.170
The tension had been so
bad that he basically

00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:17.900
lost it afterwards.

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:22.010
And, of course, he lost
more battles than he won.

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:24.860
But the sort of
bruising contests

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:27.080
that he was engaged in
with regard with regard

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:30.223
to Franklin Roosevelt
was one of the reasons--

00:30:30.223 --> 00:30:32.390
it's not the primary reason--
and one of the reasons

00:30:32.390 --> 00:30:36.410
why Roosevelt felt
free to call him

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:40.940
the most dangerous man in
America, along with Huey Long.

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:46.430
And it was also one reason why
Roosevelt encouraged MacArthur

00:30:46.430 --> 00:30:51.380
to take up the post of commander
in chief of Philippine forces

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:55.550
and to go to the Philippines and
resign his commission in the US

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:59.900
Army in order to take up
that post in the country

00:30:59.900 --> 00:31:02.240
that his father,
Arthur MacArthur,

00:31:02.240 --> 00:31:07.730
had helped to liberate and to
restructure after the Spanish

00:31:07.730 --> 00:31:12.290
American War and in which
he had served for many years

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:15.550
as a young officer and
then as a major general.

00:31:19.490 --> 00:31:26.090
That kind of confrontation with
superiors would continue during

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:30.980
World War II where he and
Roosevelt again had constant

00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:34.250
bruising battles over
the course of the war,

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:37.310
about whether the war
should be focused on Europe,

00:31:37.310 --> 00:31:40.820
as opposed to being
focused in Asia,

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:44.450
contests about what part of
the Pacific War it should be

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:48.800
focused on as well because the
Navy had pushing on-- you can

00:31:48.800 --> 00:31:51.230
see on the right
there, Admiral Nimitz--

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:54.950
had their own conception of how
to win the war in the Pacific

00:31:54.950 --> 00:31:56.900
and the direction
in which US forces

00:31:56.900 --> 00:32:01.400
and US resources
should be committed.

00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:03.475
And MacArthur had
very different ideas

00:32:03.475 --> 00:32:05.600
about the way in which the
war should be conducted,

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:07.370
that it should be
one that conducted

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:10.550
through the southwest
Pacific to final liberation

00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:14.930
of the Philippines and
then use the Philippines

00:32:14.930 --> 00:32:18.160
as a springboard for the
ultimate invasion of Japan

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:21.590
whereas Nimitz and the
Air Force and the Navy

00:32:21.590 --> 00:32:27.340
saw a push through the Central
Pacific that would finish up

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:30.850
in Formosa, Taiwan.

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:32.710
And then that would
become the base

00:32:32.710 --> 00:32:35.665
for eventual invasion of Japan.

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:45.240
In the end, MacArthur never
won his contest with Nimitz,

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:49.560
but he was able to ensure that
both thrusts into the Pacific

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:52.380
received equal time--
or close to equal time

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:53.790
and equal commitment.

00:32:53.790 --> 00:32:59.250
And that when the two arms
of US effort in the Pacific

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:03.750
met that he would assume
supreme command of the forces

00:33:03.750 --> 00:33:08.030
as they assembled together for
the final approach on Japan.

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:16.550
So you see the Douglas
MacArthur who would ultimately

00:33:16.550 --> 00:33:21.218
confront Harry Truman over the
conduct of the war in Korea--

00:33:21.218 --> 00:33:23.510
I'm going to say a little
bit more about that in detail

00:33:23.510 --> 00:33:24.620
in a little bit--

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:27.050
that that Douglas
MacArthur is one

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:29.810
who had already been schooled
in this approach to dealing

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:31.460
with superiors.

00:33:31.460 --> 00:33:34.730
And that is that if you know
you're right, tell them so.

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:38.050
And if you know they're wrong,
then definitely tell them so.

00:33:41.390 --> 00:33:44.450
Whether it's a general or
whether it's a president,

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:48.350
you have the privilege
and the responsibility

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:55.940
to point out their mistakes and
to tell them why you are right

00:33:55.940 --> 00:33:57.460
and why they are wrong.

00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:59.900
And that ultimate
confrontation then

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:07.790
in February of 1951 between
Truman and MacArthur

00:34:07.790 --> 00:34:09.520
has this long buildup--

00:34:09.520 --> 00:34:12.920
a long pattern of behavior
on the part of MacArthur,

00:34:12.920 --> 00:34:15.139
that as I said,
what's amazing is

00:34:15.139 --> 00:34:16.909
when you think about
someone who takes

00:34:16.909 --> 00:34:18.770
that kind of truculent
attitude with regards

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:21.560
to his bosses and
his superiors, it's

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:25.320
amazing he had the career he
did under those circumstances.

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:30.020
I think it's proof of the
degree to which even those who

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:33.500
thoroughly dislike Douglas
MacArthur had to respect him,

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:35.630
if not actually
fear him, and were

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:37.820
able to give him the
promotions and give him

00:34:37.820 --> 00:34:41.480
the responsibilities of
command that they knew,

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:44.884
really against their will, that
he was capable of fulfilling.

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:49.270
Now that landing at Inchon--

00:34:49.270 --> 00:34:51.340
the landing at
Inchon, that was again

00:34:51.340 --> 00:34:55.310
the final result of
confrontation with the White

00:34:55.310 --> 00:34:59.380
House, with Truman's senior
advisors with the Joint Chiefs

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:02.560
of Staff here really
brings us then

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:07.690
to where Douglas MacArthur's
ability to defy criticism

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:12.730
and to prevail really
reached its height because

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:18.280
of the landing of Inchon is it
led to the occupation of Seoul

00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:21.930
and then to the
liberation of Pyongyang

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:25.360
were really the result of
that final strategic move.

00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:27.722
I just put this slide up
there because I love it.

00:35:27.722 --> 00:35:29.930
It doesn't really have any
business doing it in this.

00:35:29.930 --> 00:35:33.610
But it's a wonderful
photograph of this squad

00:35:33.610 --> 00:35:36.190
of Marines in the process
of retaking Seoul.

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:39.380
That is an action
photograph, let me tell you,

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:43.030
these guys looking for looking
for North Korean snipers

00:35:43.030 --> 00:35:45.590
as they're going
through the operations

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:49.640
to free Seoul from
the communists--

00:35:49.640 --> 00:35:50.390
love that picture.

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:57.500
Now to the third reason why
Douglas MacArthur I think

00:35:57.500 --> 00:36:00.590
has been treated badly by
historians and by posterity,

00:36:00.590 --> 00:36:03.500
particularly posterity
on the left side

00:36:03.500 --> 00:36:05.100
of the political aisle.

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:08.240
And that is his
fierce anticommunism.

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:11.480
No doubt about that--
this played a big role

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:15.380
in seeing Douglas MacArthur
in a very different light

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:18.950
than other military
figures of the 20th century

00:36:18.950 --> 00:36:21.500
who have not been so
strident and have not

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:25.610
been so forthright in their
detestation of communism

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:27.200
in all of its works.

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:30.440
It was that hatred of
communism, for example,

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:32.870
that led him to take the
very strong line with regard

00:36:32.870 --> 00:36:36.560
to the Bonus Army marchers when
they descended on Washington

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:40.940
in 1932 and his decision
that they would simply not

00:36:40.940 --> 00:36:45.560
be allowed to stay and they
had to go, in large part,

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:48.860
because he feared, as
I explain in the book

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:51.770
with some justification,
that the Bonus Army

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:56.120
marchers actually had been
infiltrated by elements

00:36:56.120 --> 00:36:59.300
of the Communist Party of
the United States of America

00:36:59.300 --> 00:37:04.370
who saw that mass occupation
of the nation's capital

00:37:04.370 --> 00:37:06.620
as a prelude to
possible revolution,

00:37:06.620 --> 00:37:09.360
certainly to large-scale
street violence,

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:12.540
not just in Washington,
but around the country.

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:17.150
And so as for this
reason that MacArthur

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:20.930
believed that even though
the Bonus marchers' cause may

00:37:20.930 --> 00:37:26.210
possibly have been just
that their methods were

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:29.510
unjust and wrong and
that they therefore

00:37:29.510 --> 00:37:32.570
had to be forced
out of their camps

00:37:32.570 --> 00:37:36.950
that they had pitched
on the National Mall.

00:37:36.950 --> 00:37:39.810
Now it has to be said--
and if you read my book,

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:41.840
you'll get the
details about this--

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:44.300
that the violence
that was involved,

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:47.930
the shooting and killing
of Bonus marchers

00:37:47.930 --> 00:37:50.660
and so on was not by
MacArthur or by his troops.

00:37:50.660 --> 00:37:53.240
It was by the DC police.

00:37:53.240 --> 00:37:57.760
If any force was really out
of control in the Bonus march

00:37:57.760 --> 00:38:00.820
confrontation, it
was the DC police.

00:38:00.820 --> 00:38:02.960
It was left to
MacArthur and the Army

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:07.190
to pick up the pieces from
the mess and the debacle

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:08.750
that they had caused.

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:11.370
But it was a violent
confrontation.

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:14.150
It was one that ruined the
reputation of the Hoover

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:16.370
administration who
the White House had

00:38:16.370 --> 00:38:21.410
given the final order to
evict the Bonus Army marchers.

00:38:21.410 --> 00:38:27.560
And it almost certainly was the
final straw for finishing off

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:31.010
any chance of Hoover
being re-elected in 1932,

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:32.510
not just the
Depression, but also

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:35.390
the debacle of the
Bonus march and the way

00:38:35.390 --> 00:38:38.030
in which it was put out.

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:40.550
But MacArthur's
anti-communist statements

00:38:40.550 --> 00:38:42.230
regarding the Bonus
marchers would

00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:45.290
come to haunt him in
the leftist press.

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:49.130
And they very quickly
made him the key villain

00:38:49.130 --> 00:38:50.930
in the whole story
of the Bonus march.

00:38:50.930 --> 00:38:53.390
There were even
stories that circulated

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:57.350
in the Daily Worker-- of all
places, the communist Daily

00:38:57.350 --> 00:39:00.860
Worker-- that MacArthur
had led the charge

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:05.000
against the unarmed Bonus
marchers on a white horse

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:08.790
here in this marvelous cavalry
charge, all made up, of course.

00:39:08.790 --> 00:39:10.890
There's no truth
to it whatsoever.

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:17.000
But it made him an easy
target and a favorite target

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:18.830
for the leftist press.

00:39:18.830 --> 00:39:22.580
That was going to get worse,
of course, after his dismissal

00:39:22.580 --> 00:39:26.750
by Harry Truman in 1951
because of their disagreements

00:39:26.750 --> 00:39:30.390
on the policy in the
conduct of the war in Korea.

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:38.220
And his speech to the
joint session of Congress

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:41.550
when he returned to the
United States to rapturous

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:45.210
crowds of millions of people in
every city that he and his wife

00:39:45.210 --> 00:39:49.170
stopped in on the way back
on the way to Washington.

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:52.320
And that speech to
the joint session

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:56.700
of Congress, the famous one that
ends "old soldiers never die,"

00:39:56.700 --> 00:40:02.070
that speech contained a
strong anti-communist message

00:40:02.070 --> 00:40:06.570
and made it clear that the
Truman administration's policy

00:40:06.570 --> 00:40:10.590
was inadequate for keeping
the forces of communism

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:14.220
from being on the march in Asia.

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:17.070
And, again, it also
helped to make then

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:20.460
MacArthur a favorite
target on the liberal press

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:23.610
and liberal media and
defenders of Truman

00:40:23.610 --> 00:40:28.470
as being someone whose dismissal
was not just a matter of result

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:34.170
of disagreements over policy
in how to conduct war in Korea,

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:40.380
but had been a gross
violation of the separation

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:42.720
between military
and civilian command

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:45.720
of the armed forces here,
almost to the point of treating

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:47.430
MacArthur as if he
had almost staged

00:40:47.430 --> 00:40:51.360
a kind of coup against the
government by his refusal

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:55.380
and by his public pronouncements
about the Truman policy

00:40:55.380 --> 00:40:56.940
on the war in Korea.

00:40:56.940 --> 00:41:00.090
The Republican Congress that
took up the investigation

00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:02.550
into MacArthur's firing
probably didn't help the case

00:41:02.550 --> 00:41:06.900
very much because instead of
concentrating their efforts

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:11.430
on the merits and demerits
for MacArthur's dismissal,

00:41:11.430 --> 00:41:13.200
were obsessed with
the idea that there

00:41:13.200 --> 00:41:17.040
were secret communists
in the Truman White House

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:22.350
who had engineered this thing
at the behest of Moscow.

00:41:22.350 --> 00:41:25.300
There simply was no evidence
to support such claims.

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:28.380
But it did a lot to dissipate
the energies and the focus

00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:30.870
of those hearings on
MacArthur's dismissal

00:41:30.870 --> 00:41:33.600
and again helped to
make the Truman forces

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:36.090
and those supporters seem
more right than wrong

00:41:36.090 --> 00:41:37.970
in the course of the discussion.

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:44.100
And just that fact then led
to the other major factor

00:41:44.100 --> 00:41:49.080
in the turning of the nation's
view about Douglas MacArthur

00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:54.660
because it became an industry,
especially for historians

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:58.440
linked to Democrat party and
to the Truman administration,

00:41:58.440 --> 00:42:02.610
people like Arthur Schlesinger,
for example, to suggest

00:42:02.610 --> 00:42:06.860
that what they saw as
MacArthur's disastrous policy

00:42:06.860 --> 00:42:09.540
in Korea, which had
led to his dismissal

00:42:09.540 --> 00:42:13.620
is the charge, that
that had a long history

00:42:13.620 --> 00:42:16.740
of military failures,
stretching all the way back

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:19.620
to the Philippines when
he had been caught flat

00:42:19.620 --> 00:42:24.090
footed by the Japanese
attack on December 7--

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:30.030
time to occur at the same time
as the attack on Pearl Harbor.

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:33.960
And that a series of major
mistakes by MacArthur

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:38.010
had led to the failure and the
collapse of American resistance

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:43.350
in the Philippines of
a disgraceful episode

00:42:43.350 --> 00:42:46.740
in America's military history,
which MacArthur had fled

00:42:46.740 --> 00:42:50.370
from by escaping
from Corregidor,

00:42:50.370 --> 00:42:53.280
overlooking the fact that
MacArthur, as I explained

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:56.220
in my book, had quite set for
himself the idea that he was

00:42:56.220 --> 00:43:00.060
going to die at Corregidor,
die with the rest of his troops

00:43:00.060 --> 00:43:03.480
and that it was
Roosevelt's order

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:10.110
that he leave the Philippines
and go to Australia to assume

00:43:10.110 --> 00:43:13.770
command of American forces
to relieve the Philippines.

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:16.590
It was that direct order
which he at the end

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:21.360
decided he could not disobey
and that led him to lead himself

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:27.040
and his family and his staff
to leave the island fortress.

00:43:27.040 --> 00:43:31.050
And, of course, what's easy
to forget is that the missteps

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:35.292
and the failures that had taken
place in the Philippines were

00:43:35.292 --> 00:43:37.500
also then matched from the
point of the [? liberal ?]

00:43:37.500 --> 00:43:40.920
[? trends ?] by what had
happened in the Korea where it

00:43:40.920 --> 00:43:45.390
was argued MacArthur had failed
to understand the dangers that

00:43:45.390 --> 00:43:49.050
[? we ?] run by having US
forces advance as far north

00:43:49.050 --> 00:43:51.450
as the Yalu River
and that China--

00:43:51.450 --> 00:43:53.460
the communist China--
then in reaction

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:56.880
to that American thrust
then poured troops

00:43:56.880 --> 00:44:01.020
across the border, catching
MacArthur's forces by surprise

00:44:01.020 --> 00:44:04.500
and triggering a massive
panicked retreat to the south,

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:07.800
to the far southern
end of the peninsula

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:10.530
and leading to such
military disasters

00:44:10.530 --> 00:44:16.110
as the entrapment at
[? Chu Sin ?] at the reservoir

00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:21.090
there and the long, bloody
retreat from out of North Korea

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:22.230
into the south.

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:23.380
Here all of this--

00:44:23.380 --> 00:44:27.690
get more examples, historians
and biographers would claim

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:28.980
and critics would claim--

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:31.050
just one more example
of MacArthur's

00:44:31.050 --> 00:44:33.900
flawed military
judgment and inability

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:37.670
to learn from his own mistakes.

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:39.250
Of course, what
this overlooked was,

00:44:39.250 --> 00:44:41.690
number one, in the
case of the Philippines

00:44:41.690 --> 00:44:44.350
that MacArthur did actually
get to the Philippines,

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:48.070
did actually muster
together American forces

00:44:48.070 --> 00:44:51.670
and Australian forces to
begin the process of pushing

00:44:51.670 --> 00:44:54.010
the Japanese out of
the South Pacific,

00:44:54.010 --> 00:44:55.840
starting in New Guinea.

00:44:55.840 --> 00:44:57.400
And MacArthur,
what MacArthur was

00:44:57.400 --> 00:45:00.490
able to do was to do
this by pressing forward

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:04.270
with a new way of thinking
about military warfare,

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:07.360
and that was as what we would
call today combined arms

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:11.680
operations, involving forces
at sea, forces at land

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:15.370
and forces in the air,
a series of surprise

00:45:15.370 --> 00:45:19.090
amphibious landings
backed by airpower

00:45:19.090 --> 00:45:21.910
that would enable
the US to turn what

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:24.790
would seem to be an
insuperable obstacle

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:31.540
to coherent military operations,
this vast archipelago spread

00:45:31.540 --> 00:45:36.130
out across all of these,
this vast stretch of ocean,

00:45:36.130 --> 00:45:39.940
and to turn what would seem to
be a disadvantage for US forces

00:45:39.940 --> 00:45:41.440
into an advantage,

00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:43.900
that you could strike where
the Japanese least expected

00:45:43.900 --> 00:45:47.440
it and could overwhelm the
Japanese garrisons and islands

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:52.330
one by one, a strategy that
became known as island hopping.

00:45:52.330 --> 00:45:55.030
And in the process,
by doing so, MacArthur

00:45:55.030 --> 00:45:57.460
not only found a way in
which he could defeat

00:45:57.460 --> 00:46:00.340
the Japanese piecemeal in
ways that they could not

00:46:00.340 --> 00:46:05.050
keep up with and could not
reinforce their positions.

00:46:05.050 --> 00:46:06.550
But at the same
time, he found a way

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:08.890
in which to save American lives.

00:46:08.890 --> 00:46:12.340
That the amphibious landings,
catching the enemies

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:14.590
in the rear when
they least expected

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:19.240
it was actually a way in which
you kept casualties down,

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:21.790
compared to the full
frontal assaults that

00:46:21.790 --> 00:46:26.200
places like Tarawa and
later at Iwo Jima and then

00:46:26.200 --> 00:46:28.570
later at Okinawa.

00:46:28.570 --> 00:46:30.400
And we have to say
one of the reasons

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:34.450
why MacArthur was so
well liked by his troops

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:39.160
and by his junior officers was
he kept him alive at a time

00:46:39.160 --> 00:46:43.060
when many of their fellow
Marines or many Army

00:46:43.060 --> 00:46:46.270
fellow Army officers and
soldiers were being killed

00:46:46.270 --> 00:46:50.020
in large numbers in the Central
Pacific campaigns here--

00:46:50.020 --> 00:46:53.050
highly successful
operational strategy

00:46:53.050 --> 00:46:55.180
that also turned
into one that could

00:46:55.180 --> 00:46:59.440
be used to reduce American
casualties while accelerating

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:00.400
Japanese ones.

00:47:03.180 --> 00:47:05.400
And then, of course,
that also set the stage--

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:08.255
that campaign in the
Pacific and the route

00:47:08.255 --> 00:47:09.630
that he had taken
in order to get

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:11.910
there with the liberation
of the Philippines, first

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:13.320
and foremost in his mind--

00:47:13.320 --> 00:47:15.700
also set the stage
for the next stage,

00:47:15.700 --> 00:47:19.920
which was the occupation
and liberation of Japan,

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:25.470
for giving Japan a new reason
for life as a civilized

00:47:25.470 --> 00:47:29.670
community, and this photograph--
the photograph of MacArthur

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:33.630
in his basically Army fatigues--

00:47:33.630 --> 00:47:35.340
by the way, the
uniform he always

00:47:35.340 --> 00:47:37.650
wore without decorations,
with just a mark,

00:47:37.650 --> 00:47:40.710
just the insignia of
his rank, standing

00:47:40.710 --> 00:47:47.490
next to the Emperor
Hirohito, a god to his people

00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:52.140
before and during the war,
sent shockwaves across Japan

00:47:52.140 --> 00:47:54.330
when that photograph appeared.

00:47:54.330 --> 00:48:00.090
It was as shocking as it would
be to see an American president

00:48:00.090 --> 00:48:04.770
standing next to a Martian,
which is, in effect, what

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:07.800
Americans were from the point
of view of the Japanese.

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:10.830
So remote was their culture,
so remote was their country

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:12.330
and values here.

00:48:12.330 --> 00:48:14.340
It was a real signal
that a new era

00:48:14.340 --> 00:48:16.680
has come, for better or
for worse, for Japan.

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:20.400
You better get used to it
because the emperor has already

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:23.230
gotten used to it and realized
this is the new existence.

00:48:23.230 --> 00:48:26.010
This is the new Japan of
which I'm going to be part.

00:48:26.010 --> 00:48:29.520
And within two years,
the Japanese people

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:33.300
came to realize that MacArthur,
far from being a conqueror,

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:35.190
was, in fact, a
liberator, that he

00:48:35.190 --> 00:48:37.690
was a man who had brought
a new era to Japan

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:41.920
and a new era for Asia as
a whole in the process.

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:45.420
And when you go to Tokyo and
you go to [INAUDIBLE] insurance

00:48:45.420 --> 00:48:47.403
building, which is where
he was headquartered--

00:48:47.403 --> 00:48:49.320
the old insurance building
is gone, of course.

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:51.060
It's the modern one.

00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:53.300
Up in the floor,
they have preserved

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:56.880
and have reconstructed
MacArthur's office there--

00:48:56.880 --> 00:49:00.150
really a tribute
to a figure who is,

00:49:00.150 --> 00:49:04.110
I would say, much
more a hero in Japan

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:06.720
than he is here,
really, in many ways,

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:09.060
just as he is as
much more of a hero

00:49:09.060 --> 00:49:11.280
in Korea because
of his liberation

00:49:11.280 --> 00:49:14.790
of Seoul during the Korean
War than he is here as well.

00:49:14.790 --> 00:49:15.990
It's ironic.

00:49:15.990 --> 00:49:17.700
It's ironic-- an
American general

00:49:17.700 --> 00:49:20.170
who was celebrated
more by foreigners

00:49:20.170 --> 00:49:24.095
to appreciate what he did
than he is by Americans.

00:49:24.095 --> 00:49:25.470
And what I also
have to say about

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:26.970
him too is that,
in those years, we

00:49:26.970 --> 00:49:29.640
come to realize that the
person who really made all

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:32.190
of this possible for MacArthur,
who really held him together

00:49:32.190 --> 00:49:35.790
for all those years of
strife and of tension

00:49:35.790 --> 00:49:39.390
was his wife, Jean
MacArthur, amazing woman

00:49:39.390 --> 00:49:43.920
from Murfreesboro, Tennessee,
his second wife and the one

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:46.800
with whom he finally found
domestic peace and domestic

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:48.380
support all through his career.

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:56.310
She is, I think, one of the
key factors in how MacArthur

00:49:56.310 --> 00:49:59.760
was able to function with that
degree of confidence and skill

00:49:59.760 --> 00:50:02.700
through, say, all the ups
and downs of the career that

00:50:02.700 --> 00:50:03.620
went with it.

00:50:03.620 --> 00:50:05.520
And when I wrote
the biography, I

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:07.830
had the privilege of
being the first historian

00:50:07.830 --> 00:50:13.560
to use her oral biography in the
archives as part of the book.

00:50:13.560 --> 00:50:16.715
It was new material-- brand
new unreleased material,

00:50:16.715 --> 00:50:18.840
which I was able to
incorporate into the biography.

00:50:18.840 --> 00:50:20.933
And it was a real
privilege to be

00:50:20.933 --> 00:50:22.350
able to work through
that material

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:25.710
and to integrate it into it.

00:50:25.710 --> 00:50:29.010
Now [? I should ?] say a final
word about the other aspect

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:31.320
of Douglas MacArthur
that makes him,

00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:35.310
I think, also unique among
American generals perhaps

00:50:35.310 --> 00:50:38.045
and also certainly among
American political figures.

00:50:38.045 --> 00:50:39.420
And that was the
degree of vision

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:42.630
that he brought to his
work and to his value.

00:50:42.630 --> 00:50:46.230
He thought of himself not simply
as a general in military terms,

00:50:46.230 --> 00:50:49.320
but generals in the
broadest strategic terms

00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:51.240
at the same time here.

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:53.790
And what I have to say is
that, in many ways MacArthur,

00:50:53.790 --> 00:50:55.800
when we look at
his career, we look

00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:58.290
at the way in which he
envisioned himself acting

00:50:58.290 --> 00:51:01.320
on the world stage, which he
became accustomed to at a very

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:06.410
young age with his experience
during the First World War

00:51:06.410 --> 00:51:09.510
and after that in
many ways he was

00:51:09.510 --> 00:51:12.600
a person who saw the
future more clearly than he

00:51:12.600 --> 00:51:14.610
saw the present.

00:51:14.610 --> 00:51:17.780
And that's a quality, I
think, that historians

00:51:17.780 --> 00:51:21.550
can come to recognize that
perhaps sometimes escapes

00:51:21.550 --> 00:51:22.970
contemporaries.

00:51:22.970 --> 00:51:25.880
And I think the best
example of this in many ways

00:51:25.880 --> 00:51:29.510
is the way in which he came to
understand the forces at work

00:51:29.510 --> 00:51:31.780
in dynamics in
world politics that

00:51:31.780 --> 00:51:33.170
were at work in the Korean War.

00:51:36.020 --> 00:51:40.490
For Truman and his
policymakers, the Korean War

00:51:40.490 --> 00:51:43.400
was very much seen
through the prism

00:51:43.400 --> 00:51:46.010
of the conflict between the
free world and the communism--

00:51:46.010 --> 00:51:48.890
not surprisingly,
not surprisingly.

00:51:48.890 --> 00:51:51.600
That's absolutely
part of the story.

00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:53.180
There's no doubt about that.

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:56.460
But MacArthur saw it in a
slightly different regard.

00:51:56.460 --> 00:52:00.230
And it was regarded that he
supplied to General Marshall

00:52:00.230 --> 00:52:03.830
when Marshall in the fall
of 1950 when it became clear

00:52:03.830 --> 00:52:06.530
that Chinese were now pouring
across the border that entered

00:52:06.530 --> 00:52:09.530
into this conflict when
Marshall wrote to him

00:52:09.530 --> 00:52:12.980
and sort of said, what do you
think the Chinese are up to?

00:52:12.980 --> 00:52:17.900
And so MacArthur
told him in a cable--

00:52:17.900 --> 00:52:21.020
the cable, which I found
in the MacArthur archives.

00:52:21.020 --> 00:52:23.210
It's not been published
before but which

00:52:23.210 --> 00:52:25.427
I talk about in the book.

00:52:25.427 --> 00:52:27.510
And here's what he says
about the Chinese entrance

00:52:27.510 --> 00:52:29.467
into the war in Korea.

00:52:29.467 --> 00:52:31.050
He says, there are
activities in Korea

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:34.920
throughout have been
offensive, never defensive--

00:52:34.920 --> 00:52:37.620
blows away the whole argument
that the reason the Chinese

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:40.170
had entered the conflict was
because the Americans got

00:52:40.170 --> 00:52:41.910
too close to the Yalu River.

00:52:41.910 --> 00:52:43.800
And now, by the
way, subsequently

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:45.600
looking through
Chinese archives,

00:52:45.600 --> 00:52:47.220
we know that this was true.

00:52:47.220 --> 00:52:49.500
Mao was preparing for
this war the moment

00:52:49.500 --> 00:52:54.060
the very first American troops
arrived in the Korean peninsula

00:52:54.060 --> 00:52:55.560
in August of 1950.

00:52:55.560 --> 00:52:57.450
He was ready to
go to war already

00:52:57.450 --> 00:53:00.260
because he saw this as a
huge opportunity for China

00:53:00.260 --> 00:53:03.460
and for what China was
going to accomplish.

00:53:03.460 --> 00:53:06.090
So what is on display
in Korea, MacArthur

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:09.510
wrote, is a Chinese
nationalism of increasingly

00:53:09.510 --> 00:53:12.630
dominant regressive
tendencies that

00:53:12.630 --> 00:53:16.740
was spawned 50 years before
during the Boxer Rebellion

00:53:16.740 --> 00:53:19.380
and has been brought to
its greatest fruition

00:53:19.380 --> 00:53:22.650
under the present Mao regime.

00:53:22.650 --> 00:53:24.990
The result, he said,
has been the creation

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:28.470
of a new and dominant
power in Asia, which

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:32.550
for its own purposes is
Allied with the Soviet Union

00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:35.820
but which in its own
concepts and methods

00:53:35.820 --> 00:53:38.580
has become increasingly
and aggressively

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:42.840
imperialistic with
a lust for expansion

00:53:42.840 --> 00:53:45.350
and for increased power.

00:53:45.350 --> 00:53:49.160
He said, it's true that China's
aims are now currently aligned

00:53:49.160 --> 00:53:53.180
with that of the Soviet
Union, but the aggression

00:53:53.180 --> 00:53:56.960
shown in Korea, as well
as Indo China and Tibet,

00:53:56.960 --> 00:54:00.380
says that Peking was
following its own line

00:54:00.380 --> 00:54:03.515
of expansionist conquest.

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:09.330
On the one hand, it showed the
Chinese were not automatically

00:54:09.330 --> 00:54:12.900
Soviet satellites, as so many
in the Truman administration

00:54:12.900 --> 00:54:14.340
had assumed.

00:54:14.340 --> 00:54:17.010
On the other hand,
MacArthur wrote,

00:54:17.010 --> 00:54:23.940
when they reach the fullness
of their military potential.

00:54:23.940 --> 00:54:26.800
I dread to think
what may happen.

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:31.350
Now, I don't think you can find
anyone in American politics

00:54:31.350 --> 00:54:35.070
anywhere in 1950 who
would be able to see

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:39.780
with such prescience the
conflict in which we live today

00:54:39.780 --> 00:54:42.450
and be aware of how
aware it was coming

00:54:42.450 --> 00:54:45.470
and how it was
coming about here.

00:54:45.470 --> 00:54:47.950
And in fact, when we think
about it in that light,

00:54:47.950 --> 00:54:52.890
we can see that even now
today, China's first island

00:54:52.890 --> 00:54:56.130
chain strategy that it
has developed as a means

00:54:56.130 --> 00:54:59.310
by which to break out
from the Eurasian landmass

00:54:59.310 --> 00:55:04.120
and to become a dominant
player in world politics.

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:10.400
The first island chain
that we can see here

00:55:10.400 --> 00:55:14.420
extending from Japan all the
way down to the northern Borneo,

00:55:14.420 --> 00:55:18.620
the Philippines, the second
island chain that extend out

00:55:18.620 --> 00:55:22.500
as far as Guam to New Guinea.

00:55:22.500 --> 00:55:25.590
Their whole idea of the
island chain strategy,

00:55:25.590 --> 00:55:27.360
they stole from MacArthur.

00:55:27.360 --> 00:55:32.040
He was the one who developed
that during the Korean War,

00:55:32.040 --> 00:55:33.750
that the way in which
to contain China

00:55:33.750 --> 00:55:37.020
was by a strong US presence
and a string of bases

00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:41.310
that would hold together what he
called the first island chain.

00:55:41.310 --> 00:55:42.900
And what the Chinese
simply did was

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:46.380
to steal MacArthur's
terminology and his strategy

00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:47.640
and turn it inside out.

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:49.890
Instead of the first island
chain being a way in which

00:55:49.890 --> 00:55:52.710
to [? him ?] China in contain
it, becomes a way for China

00:55:52.710 --> 00:55:56.970
to break out and to become the
dominant power in the region

00:55:56.970 --> 00:55:58.560
and the area here.

00:55:58.560 --> 00:56:01.160
And that same strategy,
those elements

00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:05.700
that have gone on to the
rise of contemporary China--

00:56:05.700 --> 00:56:08.280
the rise of China
which envisaged itself

00:56:08.280 --> 00:56:11.280
not only as a great land power,
but now as a great maritime

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:19.210
power with a Navy able to rival
that of the United States,

00:56:19.210 --> 00:56:22.890
the Navy ready to displace
American presence, not

00:56:22.890 --> 00:56:25.110
just in the South China
Sea and the East China Sea,

00:56:25.110 --> 00:56:27.810
but across the Pacific here.

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:31.470
And even the rise of China
as a major technological and

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:34.440
high-tech power, how
it envisions itself

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:36.510
as a power in terms
of the development

00:56:36.510 --> 00:56:40.470
of artificial intelligence,
of becoming the world's

00:56:40.470 --> 00:56:45.902
leading AI-driven
country by 2025 as part

00:56:45.902 --> 00:56:46.860
of their overall plans.

00:56:46.860 --> 00:56:49.470
Their plans and quantum
technology and quantum

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:51.690
computing, which I know
a lot about these days,

00:56:51.690 --> 00:56:56.730
as a matter of fact, all of
these things are, in effect,

00:56:56.730 --> 00:57:01.230
previewed and predicted in
MacArthur's memo from October

00:57:01.230 --> 00:57:05.640
1950 here, and I think that
strategic vision and ability

00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:08.520
to contain to encapsulate
and understand

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:10.200
that strategic vision.

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:14.520
here is, I think, what puts
MacArthur in a class by himself

00:57:14.520 --> 00:57:15.550
here.

00:57:15.550 --> 00:57:18.240
And just as I think
MacArthur clearly

00:57:18.240 --> 00:57:21.900
saw the forces at work, the
correlation of forces at work,

00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:24.060
in the conflict
in Korea, I think

00:57:24.060 --> 00:57:27.000
he is a figure who if
he were with us today,

00:57:27.000 --> 00:57:30.570
here today, would understand the
correlations of forces at work

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:33.990
today in terms of the
competition between the US

00:57:33.990 --> 00:57:35.670
and China.

00:57:35.670 --> 00:57:38.440
I think we miss him
more than we realize.

00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:44.140
And we miss his vision perhaps
more than we can contemplate.

00:57:44.140 --> 00:57:47.420
But the historical
record is clear.

00:57:47.420 --> 00:57:53.270
The man stands above the critics
and above the detractors.

00:57:53.270 --> 00:57:57.950
His record stands I think
as one which reaches,

00:57:57.950 --> 00:58:00.290
in terms of its global
reach, surpasses

00:58:00.290 --> 00:58:04.650
that of any other
American statesman, let

00:58:04.650 --> 00:58:06.920
alone American general.

00:58:06.920 --> 00:58:10.950
And I think it's one why
he very much believes

00:58:10.950 --> 00:58:15.630
belongs in the select company
of great American generals

00:58:15.630 --> 00:58:18.690
and leaders that we've been
talking about all week.

00:58:18.690 --> 00:58:19.680
I'm actually done.

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:21.120
So we're all set to go.

00:58:21.120 --> 00:58:22.726
Thank you.

00:58:22.726 --> 00:58:27.984
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:31.340 --> 00:58:33.800
So we want to clear some
time for some questions,

00:58:33.800 --> 00:58:35.480
and I'm happy to field them.

00:58:35.480 --> 00:58:37.910
I think what you have to do
is they want you to go up

00:58:37.910 --> 00:58:41.180
to the people holding-- the
students holding a microphone,

00:58:41.180 --> 00:58:44.568
and then you can
shoot away, fire away.

00:58:44.568 --> 00:58:45.860
SPEAKER: Thank you, Dr. Herman.

00:58:45.860 --> 00:58:47.720
Just a quick announcement--

00:58:47.720 --> 00:58:52.010
Dr. Herman will be signing
a copy of his book, Douglas

00:58:52.010 --> 00:58:52.520
MacArthur--

00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:55.850
American Warrior in the Searle
lobby immediately following

00:58:55.850 --> 00:58:56.670
the lecture.

00:58:56.670 --> 00:58:59.110
And we now have time
for a few questions.

00:58:59.110 --> 00:59:00.360
So if you please step forward.

00:59:00.360 --> 00:59:01.620
ARTHUR HERMAN: I'm not going
to leave you with this slide,

00:59:01.620 --> 00:59:02.120
by the way.

00:59:02.120 --> 00:59:05.178
Let's get the man himself
up there, shall we?

00:59:05.178 --> 00:59:06.220
Much better, much better.

00:59:11.530 --> 00:59:12.780
AUDIENCE: Good afternoon, sir.

00:59:12.780 --> 00:59:14.010
Thank you very
much for your time

00:59:14.010 --> 00:59:15.468
coming out here to
Hillsdale today.

00:59:15.468 --> 00:59:17.140
I greatly enjoyed your lecture.

00:59:17.140 --> 00:59:17.490
ARTHUR HERMAN: Thank you.

00:59:17.490 --> 00:59:19.657
AUDIENCE: I did have a
question about a comment made

00:59:19.657 --> 00:59:23.310
regarding the William Manchester
biography on American Caesar.

00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:25.500
Manchester, in that
book, specifically

00:59:25.500 --> 00:59:28.350
makes a comment about his
relationship with the Marine

00:59:28.350 --> 00:59:30.390
Corps during the
Pacific campaign.

00:59:30.390 --> 00:59:32.520
And correct me if I'm
wrong on the account.

00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:35.340
I believe Manchester makes the
comment that MacArthur omitted

00:59:35.340 --> 00:59:38.820
Marines from unit citations
that were under his command

00:59:38.820 --> 00:59:42.390
during the Pacific campaign,
specifically making the comment

00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:45.480
and I quote, "they had
their Belleau Wood."

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:47.400
Would you be able
to confirm or debunk

00:59:47.400 --> 00:59:49.320
if there is any truth
in that and provide

00:59:49.320 --> 00:59:51.857
any further comment on his
relationship with the Marines?

00:59:51.857 --> 00:59:53.690
ARTHUR HERMAN: No, I
don't think that that's

00:59:53.690 --> 00:59:56.680
there's any kind of great
accuracy to that at all.

00:59:56.680 --> 01:00:01.110
I think that the difficulties
with the Marines that he had--

01:00:01.110 --> 01:00:04.980
and they carried out a number
of important and really quite

01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:10.080
brutal operations, particularly
at Cape Gloucester--

01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:12.960
really had to do with
disagreements between MacArthur

01:00:12.960 --> 01:00:16.830
and the Marine commanders.

01:00:16.830 --> 01:00:17.550
He's an Army guy.

01:00:17.550 --> 01:00:18.258
What do you want?

01:00:18.258 --> 01:00:24.570
The US Army was his service--

01:00:24.570 --> 01:00:28.120
in his mind the service
to be supported.

01:00:28.120 --> 01:00:30.990
The Marines were, from that
point of view, of course,

01:00:30.990 --> 01:00:33.943
not as important in
the overall story.

01:00:33.943 --> 01:00:36.360
I think the other problem that
he had with the Marines too

01:00:36.360 --> 01:00:40.020
is that is that so much of
the resources that he did

01:00:40.020 --> 01:00:42.270
he believed he should have
had from the Marine Corps

01:00:42.270 --> 01:00:46.380
was all diverted into
the Central Pacific

01:00:46.380 --> 01:00:47.730
and was sent away there.

01:00:47.730 --> 01:00:50.670
I also don't necessarily trust
Manchester's account of this.

01:00:50.670 --> 01:00:53.430
Manchester himself
was a US Marine.

01:00:53.430 --> 01:00:55.110
So I'm sorry.

01:00:55.110 --> 01:00:57.570
I think he carries a
certain amount of bias

01:00:57.570 --> 01:01:03.240
in that regard in terms of
the development between it.

01:01:03.240 --> 01:01:05.030
You just saw my
picture of the man.

01:01:05.030 --> 01:01:08.400
He's going to have abrasive
relations with everyone.

01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:09.900
If you were to turn
to the accounts,

01:01:09.900 --> 01:01:12.450
as I do in my biography,
of Australian commanders

01:01:12.450 --> 01:01:16.320
in dealing with him too, they
were very, very, very, very

01:01:16.320 --> 01:01:18.960
friction-bound in the
dealings [? with it. ?]

01:01:18.960 --> 01:01:21.300
The Australians too often
complained about the fact

01:01:21.300 --> 01:01:25.950
that MacArthur took
away their autonomy,

01:01:25.950 --> 01:01:31.890
threw them away in
sideshow operations,

01:01:31.890 --> 01:01:35.010
didn't get their share of
declarations, and so on.

01:01:35.010 --> 01:01:36.510
It's interesting
how that constantly

01:01:36.510 --> 01:01:40.020
pops up in the case of
MacArthur that there's

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:43.380
so much controversy surrounding
the issues about decorations

01:01:43.380 --> 01:01:45.960
and things like that.

01:01:45.960 --> 01:01:48.870
But the Manchester
biography, that's

01:01:48.870 --> 01:01:51.800
one of the reasons why
I wanted to replace it

01:01:51.800 --> 01:01:53.515
and to supplant it.

01:01:53.515 --> 01:01:55.140
The other reasons,
I think the chapters

01:01:55.140 --> 01:01:57.250
that he has on the
Korean War there

01:01:57.250 --> 01:02:01.860
are really inadequate as
well as totally misguided.

01:02:01.860 --> 01:02:04.300
There's a reason for
that, by the way.

01:02:04.300 --> 01:02:08.640
And that is that the masterful
biography, the biography

01:02:08.640 --> 01:02:11.400
of MacArthur, the
three-volume one by D. Clayton

01:02:11.400 --> 01:02:15.240
James, the third volume,
which is on Korea,

01:02:15.240 --> 01:02:18.960
hadn't been finished by the
time Manchester got started

01:02:18.960 --> 01:02:22.320
on his biography, wasn't done.

01:02:22.320 --> 01:02:24.480
So he didn't have
that to lean on

01:02:24.480 --> 01:02:27.040
in terms of his discussion
about documentation

01:02:27.040 --> 01:02:30.630
and about the overall
course of the war.

01:02:30.630 --> 01:02:32.640
He was sort of left
to his own devices.

01:02:32.640 --> 01:02:35.160
And I'm afraid his
own devices were not

01:02:35.160 --> 01:02:37.560
really adequate to the job
when it came to writing

01:02:37.560 --> 01:02:40.400
about MacArthur, just my view.

01:02:40.400 --> 01:02:44.110
Sir, what else?

01:02:44.110 --> 01:02:46.060
SPEAKER: We have time
for one more question.

01:02:46.060 --> 01:02:48.866
ARTHUR HERMAN: Just one more?

01:02:48.866 --> 01:02:50.750
SPEAKER: We have
time for two more.

01:02:50.750 --> 01:02:52.000
ARTHUR HERMAN: How about that?

01:02:52.000 --> 01:02:54.610
Give me another
[? turn then-- ?] yeah, please.

01:02:54.610 --> 01:02:56.470
SPEAKER: I was interested
in your comment

01:02:56.470 --> 01:02:59.500
about his ability to
see into the future.

01:02:59.500 --> 01:03:01.900
My father flew
B-29s in the Pacific

01:03:01.900 --> 01:03:04.930
during World War II as a
member of the First Photo

01:03:04.930 --> 01:03:06.430
Reconnaissance Squadron.

01:03:06.430 --> 01:03:08.260
And he always told
me that he thought

01:03:08.260 --> 01:03:11.740
MacArthur had great foresight
because, at the end of the war,

01:03:11.740 --> 01:03:14.030
before they were
allowed to come home,

01:03:14.030 --> 01:03:18.310
they were ordered to fly
mapping grids over all of Korea

01:03:18.310 --> 01:03:20.470
to map all of Korea.

01:03:20.470 --> 01:03:23.050
And I was wondering if
you would comment on

01:03:23.050 --> 01:03:25.270
whether you thought even
at that point in time

01:03:25.270 --> 01:03:28.223
that MacArthur was foreseeing
that there was going

01:03:28.223 --> 01:03:29.890
to be great trouble
there because that's

01:03:29.890 --> 01:03:31.315
what my father always thought?

01:03:31.315 --> 01:03:33.190
ARTHUR HERMAN: Well,
that's very interesting.

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:34.310
I don't know.

01:03:34.310 --> 01:03:38.410
I couldn't tell you if that
orders were coming directly

01:03:38.410 --> 01:03:40.870
from MacArthur himself.

01:03:40.870 --> 01:03:44.440
You have to understand
that Korea was outside

01:03:44.440 --> 01:03:47.530
of his perimeter
of responsibility

01:03:47.530 --> 01:03:50.470
when he was supreme
commander and then afterwards

01:03:50.470 --> 01:03:55.990
as head of the Allied
occupation forces in Japan.

01:03:55.990 --> 01:03:58.448
The State Department
was in charge.

01:03:58.448 --> 01:03:59.740
Most people don't realize that.

01:03:59.740 --> 01:04:02.590
State Department was in
charge of postwar Korea,

01:04:02.590 --> 01:04:05.902
not MacArthur or the US Army.

01:04:05.902 --> 01:04:07.360
There was a military
mission there,

01:04:07.360 --> 01:04:09.970
but it was subordinate
to the State Department's

01:04:09.970 --> 01:04:10.990
responsibility.

01:04:13.970 --> 01:04:15.480
I'll tell you this--

01:04:15.480 --> 01:04:18.730
and this perhaps is collateral
for your father's theory--

01:04:18.730 --> 01:04:22.960
if MacArthur had been placed
in command of Korea as well

01:04:22.960 --> 01:04:26.620
as Japan, if it had
been incorporated

01:04:26.620 --> 01:04:29.590
into his Area Of
Responsibilities,

01:04:29.590 --> 01:04:34.500
his AOR, maybe the Korean
War may never have happened.

01:04:34.500 --> 01:04:36.160
It's quite possible
that he may have

01:04:36.160 --> 01:04:39.460
been able to take steps
that would have made

01:04:39.460 --> 01:04:42.790
a North Korean attack less
likely and certainly less

01:04:42.790 --> 01:04:44.500
devastating than
it actually was.

01:04:48.740 --> 01:04:49.960
AUDIENCE: On Okinawa?

01:04:49.960 --> 01:04:51.070
ARTHUR HERMAN: Yeah.

01:04:51.070 --> 01:04:53.770
Yeah, there he would have
been in MacArthur's AOR.

01:04:53.770 --> 01:04:55.300
There's no doubt about that.

01:04:55.300 --> 01:04:56.460
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

01:04:56.460 --> 01:04:56.800
AUDIENCE: Yes, sir.

01:04:56.800 --> 01:04:58.630
I was wondering if you
could talk on the perceived

01:04:58.630 --> 01:05:01.090
instability by the Joint
Chiefs of Staff in Washington

01:05:01.090 --> 01:05:03.190
on MacArthur towards the
end of the Korean War

01:05:03.190 --> 01:05:05.890
and on top of that, some
interviews that he gave

01:05:05.890 --> 01:05:10.030
after the war in the
mid-1950s about how he claimed

01:05:10.030 --> 01:05:12.880
his plan was to beat
back China by using up

01:05:12.880 --> 01:05:16.120
to 30 to 50 nuclear weapons
in the actual conflict that

01:05:16.120 --> 01:05:19.690
proceeded in China
and some weird talk

01:05:19.690 --> 01:05:22.300
that he gave about possibly
contaminating the land

01:05:22.300 --> 01:05:24.838
area to prevent the ease of
movement by the Chinese Army.

01:05:24.838 --> 01:05:26.380
And I was wondering
if you could talk

01:05:26.380 --> 01:05:28.410
about that kind of mentality?

01:05:28.410 --> 01:05:30.160
Because I think that's
why a lot of people

01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:32.680
look pretty unfavorably on him
towards the end of his career.

01:05:32.680 --> 01:05:33.650
ARTHUR HERMAN:
Yeah, that's right.

01:05:33.650 --> 01:05:35.567
I think there's also a
lot of misunderstanding

01:05:35.567 --> 01:05:36.302
surrounding that.

01:05:36.302 --> 01:05:37.760
So I'm glad you
asked the question.

01:05:37.760 --> 01:05:41.640
I can somewhat set the
record straight on that.

01:05:41.640 --> 01:05:47.410
The issue of the use of nuclear
weapons in the Korean conflict,

01:05:47.410 --> 01:05:51.310
we don't really know what
MacArthur's plan was while he

01:05:51.310 --> 01:05:54.610
was commander of UN forces.

01:05:54.610 --> 01:05:56.595
He had put that on the
table as an option,

01:05:56.595 --> 01:05:57.970
but it was an
option that already

01:05:57.970 --> 01:06:01.630
existed in which the
Joint Chiefs of Staff

01:06:01.630 --> 01:06:04.810
had already prepared for.

01:06:04.810 --> 01:06:06.850
Everyone understood
that nuclear weapons

01:06:06.850 --> 01:06:09.340
were a devastating weapon.

01:06:09.340 --> 01:06:11.950
The idea of using
nuclear weapons

01:06:11.950 --> 01:06:15.040
to attack Chinese cities
had absolutely no appeal

01:06:15.040 --> 01:06:15.860
for MacArthur.

01:06:15.860 --> 01:06:17.230
What was the point?

01:06:17.230 --> 01:06:18.940
The point of it
was that you would

01:06:18.940 --> 01:06:22.450
use nuclear weapons to create--

01:06:22.450 --> 01:06:25.780
I'm going to use the
French, [FRENCH],,

01:06:25.780 --> 01:06:30.010
an impenetrable barrier in
which Chinese reinforcements

01:06:30.010 --> 01:06:34.300
for their forces in Korea
could not get through.

01:06:34.300 --> 01:06:38.050
That was part of standard
Joint Chiefs doctrine.

01:06:38.050 --> 01:06:40.630
Everybody had talked about
this as a possibility

01:06:40.630 --> 01:06:41.880
and the use of this.

01:06:41.880 --> 01:06:44.410
And MacArthur thought
that this could possibly

01:06:44.410 --> 01:06:47.440
be an option in that conflict.

01:06:47.440 --> 01:06:49.450
And the Joint Chiefs agreed.

01:06:49.450 --> 01:06:52.270
One of the declassified
documents that I got to see

01:06:52.270 --> 01:06:56.530
was one that involved
the actual flying out

01:06:56.530 --> 01:07:00.970
of components for an
atomic bomb to Tian Yang

01:07:00.970 --> 01:07:04.870
before MacArthur was fired
for that very possibility

01:07:04.870 --> 01:07:10.350
of the use of nuclear
weapons against China.

01:07:10.350 --> 01:07:15.320
Five years after Hiroshima
and Nagasaki of an event,

01:07:15.320 --> 01:07:17.410
which was seen as
having decisively

01:07:17.410 --> 01:07:19.840
knocked Japan out of
the war, it seemed

01:07:19.840 --> 01:07:21.760
to be not the great
horrific weapon

01:07:21.760 --> 01:07:23.980
that it's come to be
in modern imaginations.

01:07:23.980 --> 01:07:27.460
In 1950, it seemed a
decisive weapon of war.

01:07:27.460 --> 01:07:28.540
So you contemplated that.

01:07:28.540 --> 01:07:34.180
You contemplate that as a
battlefield theater weapon--

01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:36.610
nothing unusual about that.

01:07:36.610 --> 01:07:39.070
So MacArthur's firing,
it wasn't for threatening

01:07:39.070 --> 01:07:40.360
to use nuclear weapons.

01:07:40.360 --> 01:07:41.440
Everybody accepted that.

01:07:41.440 --> 01:07:43.900
That would have been fair game.

01:07:43.900 --> 01:07:46.810
It wasn't really even for any
for disagreeing, disobeying

01:07:46.810 --> 01:07:48.530
orders, or anything like that.

01:07:48.530 --> 01:07:51.710
It was for a letter they had
sent to the Republican Speaker

01:07:51.710 --> 01:07:54.190
of the House, Joe
Martin, complaining

01:07:54.190 --> 01:07:57.430
about the failure of the
Truman administration

01:07:57.430 --> 01:08:00.910
to fully support
his policy in Korea,

01:08:00.910 --> 01:08:04.820
which was to push the
Chinese back to the border,

01:08:04.820 --> 01:08:09.730
to push them out of
North Korea altogether.

01:08:09.730 --> 01:08:16.649
Martin read the letter
without MacArthur's knowledge

01:08:16.649 --> 01:08:18.880
on the Florida house.

01:08:18.880 --> 01:08:20.830
And for MacArthur
and his advisors,

01:08:20.830 --> 01:08:22.050
that was the last straw.

01:08:22.050 --> 01:08:25.600
He had already made statements,
pronouncements to the press,

01:08:25.600 --> 01:08:28.180
talking complaining bitterly
about the way the Truman

01:08:28.180 --> 01:08:30.550
administration
had held him back,

01:08:30.550 --> 01:08:35.359
and had stayed his hand
in operations in Korea.

01:08:35.359 --> 01:08:38.740
And so the feeling was
that this constituted

01:08:38.740 --> 01:08:41.062
a form of insubordination
that they were simply

01:08:41.062 --> 01:08:42.229
we're not going to tolerate.

01:08:42.229 --> 01:08:44.430
And so he was gone.

01:08:44.430 --> 01:08:46.790
Was it insubordination?

01:08:46.790 --> 01:08:49.689
I conclude in the
book that it was.

01:08:49.689 --> 01:08:51.910
And I conclude in the
book in the final analysis

01:08:51.910 --> 01:08:55.310
that probably MacArthur
deserved to be fired.

01:08:55.310 --> 01:08:57.170
I think he knew he
was going to be fired.

01:08:57.170 --> 01:08:59.670
That's one of the reasons why
he wrote the letter to Martin.

01:08:59.670 --> 01:09:00.700
He knew it was coming.

01:09:00.700 --> 01:09:02.229
But it had nothing to
do with nuclear weapons,

01:09:02.229 --> 01:09:04.420
and nothing to do with
quote "widening the war,

01:09:04.420 --> 01:09:05.680
in that sense."

01:09:05.680 --> 01:09:08.800
It had to do with
instead of the fear

01:09:08.800 --> 01:09:12.670
that a renewed push
to the Chinese border

01:09:12.670 --> 01:09:16.018
would simply bring more Chinese
troops into the conflict.

01:09:16.018 --> 01:09:17.560
I don't know if
that's actually true.

01:09:17.560 --> 01:09:21.160
The Chinese were fully
committed into the war already,

01:09:21.160 --> 01:09:23.080
and MacArthur and
Ridgway's forces

01:09:23.080 --> 01:09:25.479
had devastated the
Chinese forces,

01:09:25.479 --> 01:09:30.189
perhaps as much as a
million Chinese volunteers--

01:09:30.189 --> 01:09:34.569
Chinese troops have been killed
pushing back up through South

01:09:34.569 --> 01:09:37.109
Korea, up to the 30th parallel.

01:09:37.109 --> 01:09:39.250
It had been a massive slaughter.

01:09:39.250 --> 01:09:42.910
Mao really didn't have the
kinds of effective reserves

01:09:42.910 --> 01:09:47.080
to commit to a conflict
of this kind here as well.

01:09:47.080 --> 01:09:49.240
Ridgway agreed, by the
way with MacArthur.

01:09:49.240 --> 01:09:51.237
Ridgway agreed with MacArthur.

01:09:51.237 --> 01:09:53.320
That was how he envisioned
the campaign-- continue

01:09:53.320 --> 01:09:58.720
and a push all the way up
beyond the 30th parallel up

01:09:58.720 --> 01:10:02.620
to the Chinese-North Korean
border, reunify the peninsula.

01:10:02.620 --> 01:10:04.120
That was his view.

01:10:04.120 --> 01:10:06.943
And the Truman admission says
that's not going to happen.

01:10:06.943 --> 01:10:08.110
Our allies won't support it.

01:10:08.110 --> 01:10:09.910
We're stopping at
the 38th parallel.

01:10:09.910 --> 01:10:11.530
Ridgway said, that's fine.

01:10:11.530 --> 01:10:14.470
But Ridgway never
gets a reputation

01:10:14.470 --> 01:10:16.670
for being a warmonger--
quite the opposite.

01:10:16.670 --> 01:10:19.030
He is seen wrongly, as
the man who picked up

01:10:19.030 --> 01:10:20.740
the pieces that
MacArthur had left

01:10:20.740 --> 01:10:23.422
on the ground after the
Chinese attack here.

01:10:23.422 --> 01:10:24.880
I got a lot more
to say about this,

01:10:24.880 --> 01:10:26.132
but we've got more questions.

01:10:26.132 --> 01:10:28.715
SPEAKER: Actually, we don't have
time for any more questions--

01:10:28.715 --> 01:10:28.910
ARTHUR HERMAN: Yeah, I would.

01:10:28.910 --> 01:10:29.040
I would.

01:10:29.040 --> 01:10:30.457
SPEAKER: --But
please give a round

01:10:30.457 --> 01:10:31.690
of applause for Dr. Herman.

01:10:31.690 --> 01:10:32.790
Thank you so much.

01:10:32.790 --> 01:10:37.640
[APPLAUSE]

